  transcript: 'Support Ticket Triage & Insights — Discovery Call\nCustomer: Northwind Logistics\nAttendees: Priya Nair (Head of Customer Support, Northwind), Alex Chen (Solutions Architect, MongoDB)\n\nAlex: Thanks for making time. Walk me through how support tickets work today.\n\nPriya: Sure. We get about 3,000 tickets a week across email, chat, and phone,\nall logged into our helpdesk tool. Each ticket has a subject, a free-text\ndescription, the customer\'s account tier, product line, and a priority\nlevel our agents set manually. The problem is triage — a new ticket sits\nin a queue until an agent manually reads it and decides who should handle\nit and how urgent it is. That takes 10-15 minutes per ticket sometimes.\n\nAlex: What would "solved" look like?\n\nPriya: When a ticket comes in, I want the system to automatically suggest\na priority and route it to the right team, based on what\'s actually in\nthe ticket text — not just keyword rules. And when an agent opens a\nticket, I want them to immediately see 3-4 similar tickets we\'ve resolved\nbefore, with how those were resolved, so they\'re not starting from\nscratch every time.\n\nAlex: Do you have any existing categorization or tagging on old tickets?\n\nPriya: Yes — about 80,000 historical tickets, each already tagged with a\nresolution category and a resolution summary written by the agent. That\'s\nthe data we\'d want to search against for "similar past tickets."\n\nAlex: Any reporting needs beyond the individual ticket view?\n\nPriya: Definitely. I want a dashboard showing ticket volume by product\nline and priority over time, and average resolution time by team. Right\nnow I get that from a spreadsheet someone updates manually once a week,\nwhich is way too slow — I want to see it live.\n\nAlex: Who else besides you would use this day to day?\n\nPriya: Our support agents are the main users — they\'d see the queue, open\na ticket, see suggested priority and similar tickets, and mark it\nresolved. Team leads would use the dashboard. I don\'t think we need\ncustomer-facing access to this at all, this is purely internal.\n\nAlex: Any concerns about scale or performance?\n\nPriya: The historical ticket volume will keep growing — we\'re not\ndeleting anything for compliance reasons — so search over that needs to\nstay fast even as it grows into the hundreds of thousands. And the\nsimilar-ticket suggestions need to feel instant when an agent opens a\nticket, not a multi-second wait.\n\nAlex: Got it. For this proof of concept, we\'d keep it focused: import a\nrepresentative slice of your historical tickets, build the triage\nsuggestion and similar-ticket search on top of that, and a live\ndashboard. We wouldn\'t integrate with your actual helpdesk tool for this\nfirst pass — that\'d come after, if this proves out.\n\nPriya: That works for a POV. If this shows real time savings on triage, we\ncan talk about a real integration next quarter.',
  initial_spec: {
    executive_summary: 'Northwind Logistics wants an internal proof of value to reduce manual support-ticket triage effort and improve agent efficiency. Today, roughly 3,000 weekly tickets from email, chat, and phone are manually reviewed and prioritized, which can take 10–15 minutes per ticket. The desired POV should demonstrate automatic triage suggestions based on ticket text, retrieval of similar resolved historical tickets with resolution context, and a live operational dashboard for support leadership.',
    business_problem: 'Northwind’s support process relies on manual triage of incoming tickets. Agents read each ticket and decide urgency and handling team manually, creating delays and inefficiency. Reporting is also slow because leadership depends on a manually updated weekly spreadsheet instead of live operational visibility.',
    current_state: 'Northwind receives about 3,000 support tickets per week across email, chat, and phone, all logged into a helpdesk tool. Tickets include subject, free-text description, customer account tier, product line, and an agent-set priority. New tickets wait in a queue until an agent manually reads them and determines urgency and who should handle them. Historical data includes about 80,000 tickets tagged with resolution category and agent-written resolution summary. Reporting on ticket volume and resolution time is currently produced manually in a weekly spreadsheet.',
    desired_future_state: 'For the POV, Northwind wants an internal system that uses ticket content to suggest priority and routing destination, shows agents similar previously resolved tickets with resolution details when they open a ticket, and provides a live dashboard for support leaders. The POV should use a representative slice of historical tickets rather than integrating with the production helpdesk tool in the first pass.',
